WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.489
Film Courage: Markus we interviewed you in
2015 I believe?

00:00:02.489 --> 00:00:03.759
Markus Redmond, Writer/Director/Actor/Producer:
Really?

00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:07.190
Film Courage: I know it’s been a little
bit.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:08.190
Markus: Yes.

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:09.300
That’s a long time.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.900
Film Courage: It’s a long time.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:12.900
Markus: It was fun though.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:13.900
Film Courage: It was a great time.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.789
[We interviewed you] at a theater in Ventura,
CA.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:16.789
Markus: Yes.

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:20.270
Film Courage: Your interviews have many, many
views.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.270
Many, many comments.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:22.270
Markus: Really?

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:26.700
Film Courage: Some of them [the comments]
positive, some of them not so positive.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.840
I have a question I was wondering if you can
address?

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.210
And that is some of the people [commenters]
felt that you were able to get a film made

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:39.239
or sell a script because you had prior connections?

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:41.260
Can we dispel that myth?

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.420
Markus: Oh yeah…totally.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.530
I’m completely locked in because I was on
a [TV] show a long time ago so I know all

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.460
of the studio executives and I know all of
the producers and all of the investors and

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.880
they just give me money all the time so it’s
super easy.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.670
Film Courage: And you just meet them?

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:56.670
You go to Dan Tana’s for lunch?

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.970
Markus: They call me all of the time and go
“Hey Markus…we are trying to make this

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:00.970
film?

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:01.970
Would you like 30 million dollars?

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:03.899
We are going to distribute it through Warner
Brothers?”

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:05.210
And I’m like “Yeah, great.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:06.210
Sounds awesome!”

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.590
Happens all of the time.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.800
Film Courage: People who don’t live in LA…because
for Los Angeles…

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:11.800
Markus: …I’m lying.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.590
Film Courage: Yes, I can tell.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:15.590
Sarcasm.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:22.670
But some people don’t realize…they think
that you can be bartending and meet Hollywood

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.820
producers and screenwriters and get someone’s
friends script out there this way.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.630
That’s not really how it works here.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:31.150
Markus: Well here’s the thing…like…yeah…that’s
possible.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:35.869
It’s all possible.

00:01:35.869 --> 00:01:47.450
I have a friend who was a PA [production assistant]
and he was working on a studio lot and a very,

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:52.640
very, very big movie star was shooting a movie
on that lot.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:02:01.330
So everybody was super excited about it and
my friend the PA was like “Gosh I’ve written

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:06.540
this script and this movie star is perfect
for it.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:07.810
How do I find a movie star?”

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:08.810
So they couldn’t find him.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:11.170
He was always not around.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:17.650
So they figured out which car was his and
the PA put the script on the movie star’s

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:26.660
windshield and the movie star took the script
and my friend ended up being represented by

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:30.690
the movie star’s agent.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:35.130
So…yeah…all kinds of stuff can happen.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:40.560
It doesn’t mean that that is going to happen.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.020
It doesn’t mean that you are set in stone.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:50.440
Trust me, I mean I’m flattered that folks
think that I was that big of a deal on Doogie

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.260
Howser, M.D. [TV show].

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:54.260
I was not.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.660
I was like the lowest guy on the totem pole.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:02.580
I didn’t have extra connections.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.190
I was working in an industry.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:08.330
I was doing my best to pay attention to things.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:13.940
I had an agent who had no interest in my scripts.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:18.240
I think I told the story the last time we
talked [Film Courage video interview in 2015]?

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.599
I had a friend who was an actor, he had a
friend who was a manager.

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:25.709
I did not know the manager.

00:03:25.709 --> 00:03:27.840
I had written the script.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.810
My friend was like “You should meet my friend
the manager.”

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:35.370
That’s how it happened.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:45.320
I mean I guess if the concern is you had an
advantage because you had a friend, yes I

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:46.620
did.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.690
Would I have had that friend if I had lived
in Kentucky?

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:53.780
Probably not, you know what I mean?

00:03:53.780 --> 00:04:00.989
But that doesn’t mean that if you’re in
Kentucky you can’t become famous in the

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:04.800
film industry because that happens a lot,
too.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:11.161
Like people enter contests or they submit
their scripts to to the Sundance Film Festival

00:04:11.161 --> 00:04:12.200
Lab or whatever it is?

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:16.840
And they’re in Wisconsin or they’re in
Idaho or they are wherever and somebody sees

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:17.840
that script.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:22.089
They don’t have any connections, they just
have a great script.

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:27.869
And people go “We are going to fly you out
Sundance and you are going to sit here and

00:04:27.869 --> 00:04:29.629
we’re going to help you develop.”

00:04:29.629 --> 00:04:34.720
And then the film gets made and they become
instantly famous and they are sitting on the

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.419
dice and the they are making other films and
they are way past me, you know what I mean?

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:45.930
So yes, everybody has advantages or disadvantages
one way or another.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:52.830
But for the record, it’s not like I was
connected in that sense.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:58.039
Film Courage: But would you say that even
if someone is on this trajectory of film after

00:04:58.039 --> 00:05:04.539
film after film…even for that level that
it can still be like starting from scratch

00:05:04.539 --> 00:05:09.509
again for something that this person wants
to do like their own script?

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:18.590
Markus: The Industry has a very short attention
span and a long memory.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:28.129
So if they remember you well at a high enough
level there’s a lot of stuff you can try

00:05:28.129 --> 00:05:30.430
and do again.

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:37.469
I like to talk about people like Danny DeVito
or Kevin James.

00:05:37.469 --> 00:05:40.869
Everybody looks at these guys as leading men
and they are.

00:05:40.869 --> 00:05:43.240
But they didn’t start there.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:47.360
Danny DeVito was doing off-Broadway plays
playing a dog.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.889
Like literally on his hands and knees barking.

00:05:52.889 --> 00:05:55.669
But he continued to grow and got to such a
point where people loved his work that he

00:05:55.669 --> 00:05:57.009
was able to become a leading man.

00:05:57.009 --> 00:06:02.399
Same thing with Kevin James not that he was
barking on stage but he was a stand-up comedian

00:06:02.399 --> 00:06:05.659
and he was getting roles and stuff.

00:06:05.659 --> 00:06:09.949
The stand-up career got him the show.

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:15.059
He was a leading man in the show and then
he starts being a leading man in the film.

00:06:15.059 --> 00:06:21.629
So you start where you are and you grow from
there.

00:06:21.629 --> 00:06:29.249
But I think that if Kevin James had started
off with his first project like “I’m not

00:06:29.249 --> 00:06:33.159
doing this unless I’m the leading man.”

00:06:33.159 --> 00:06:36.960
I don’t necessarily think that would have
flown, you know?

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:41.130
I think you build a repertoire and people
just go with you.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.550
If people like you they will follow you anywhere.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:45.610
Look at Will Smith.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.669
Will Smith was a sitcom star right?

00:06:48.669 --> 00:06:52.219
But people loved the show and they loved him
and he was like “I want to become a serious

00:06:52.219 --> 00:06:53.219
actor.”

00:06:53.219 --> 00:06:55.649
And everybody went “Okay.”

00:06:55.649 --> 00:06:58.259
And they show up for his movies.

00:06:58.259 --> 00:07:06.629
That’s the thing, it’s not like advantages/disadvantages,
everybody’s got them.

00:07:06.629 --> 00:07:08.800
Everybody has got both.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:14.619
But it doesn’t mean that what you want is
impossible.

00:07:14.619 --> 00:07:20.520
You know…you hunker down and focus on it.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.080
None of these people are different from anybody
else.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:32.659
They are all humans, they just put their minds
in a certain space and we’re like “This

00:07:32.659 --> 00:07:33.659
is what I want.

00:07:33.659 --> 00:07:34.659
I deserve it.

00:07:34.659 --> 00:07:35.740
Nothing is going to stop me.”

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:38.719
That’s all really anybody has to do.

00:07:38.719 --> 00:07:44.020
I know this sounds “hippie dippie” or
whatever.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:48.339
But that’s all anybody did.

00:07:48.339 --> 00:07:54.869
They got rejected and had doors shut on them.

00:07:54.869 --> 00:07:58.770
And they are like “Okay, there’s another
door.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.550
I’ll go over here now.”

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:04.559
They didn’t necessarily get on a social
media platform and complain about the door

00:08:04.559 --> 00:08:07.189
that was shut on them they just kept going.

00:08:07.189 --> 00:08:12.339
That’s all you can do in my opinion.

00:08:12.339 --> 00:08:14.929
I could be wrong but that’s what I think.

